Spec: IO Module

Purpose:
  Import and export vector data between LaserReady and external formats.

Structure:
  ./svg-import.ts: Parse SVG files into IShape instances
  ./svg-export.ts: Convert shapes to SVG with mm dimensions
  ./svg-parser.ts: Low-level SVG unit conversion utilities

Owns:
  ./svg-import.ts
  ./svg-export.ts
  ./svg-parser.ts

Must:
  Sanitize all imported SVG with DOMPurify before parsing.
  Preserve physical dimensions (mm, cm, in, px) during import.
  Export SVG with width/height in mm and a viewBox in pixels.
  Apply laser mode colors as stroke in exported SVG.
  Support paths, circles, ellipses, rectangles, polygons, and compound paths.

Must not:
  Import raster images (png, jpg, bmp).
  Execute scripts embedded in SVG files.
  Modify the Zustand store directly — return shapes for the caller to store.

Depends on:
  DOMPurify
  paper.js (for path conversion)
  @PathShape
  @IShape

Scenario: import SVG with basic shapes
  Given an SVG file containing a rectangle and a circle
  When the user triggers file import
  Then 2 PathShape instances appear in the store
  And their geometry matches the SVG coordinates

Scenario: import SVG preserves physical dimensions
  Given an SVG file with width="100mm" containing a 50mm wide rect
  When the user imports the file
  Then the imported rectangle has a width of ~50mm (189px at 96DPI)

Scenario: import SVG sanitizes malicious content
  Given an SVG file containing a <script> tag and an onclick handler
  When the user imports the file
  Then the script and event handler are stripped
  And valid path geometry is still imported
  And no script executes

Scenario: export SVG with mm dimensions
  Given the canvas has 2 shapes and material is set to 300x200mm
  When the user exports as SVG
  Then the SVG has width="300mm" height="200mm"
  And shapes are rendered as <path> elements with correct coordinates
  And stroke colors match laser mode (red=cut, blue=score, black=engrave)

Scenario: SVG round-trip preserves geometry
  Given a shape is drawn, exported to SVG, and re-imported
  Then the re-imported shape matches the original within 0.1mm tolerance

Scenario: reject raster image import
  Given the user selects a .png or .jpg file for import
  Then the file is rejected (only SVG accepted)
  And a notification informs the user

Done when:
  SVG round-trip preserves shape geometry within 0.1mm tolerance.
  Malicious SVG content is stripped without crashing.
  Physical units (mm, cm, in) are correctly converted on import.
